speechtext:
House go into committee at the next sitting to consider the following proposed resolution:
That it is expedient to amend The Feeding Stuffs Act, and to provide,-
1.	That any commercial feeding stuff which does not contain as ingredients any screenings, scourings, scalp-ings, oat hulls, oat feed, buckwheat hulls, peanut hulls or shells, cottonseed hulls, peat or moss, or any other material of low feeding value, which the minister shall have power to designate by regulation, may contain a maximum of fifty per cent by weight of bran, shorts, middlings, or feed flour, singly or combined;
2.	That wheat flour sold for feeding purposes shall be deemed to be feed flour;
3.	That any material, including the scourings or scalpings in addition to all or part of the mill screenings, which has been removed from wheat in preparing such wheat for the processes employed in flour extraction, and which material contains more than eight per cent crude fibre, may be registered and sold as a commercial feeding stuff under the name "Mill Scalpings"; or, if the material has been graded by a grain inspector operating under The Canada Grain Act, it may be sold under such other name as such grain inspector may designate on his certificate of inspection.
Motion agreed to.

Notwithstanding all that the minister has said, I cannot see that an ele-
[Mr. Millar.}
vator at that port is at all needed. Since the Vancouver port has been developed for the handling of grain, very little grain has gone east after navigation has closed. And even if it were necessary to haul a part of the western grain crop to a Maritime port, what is wrong with the port of St. John? Surely that has never been congested yet, and there are no indications that it ever will be. Seeing that Vancouver is a port through which grain can be shipped out all the year round there will be absolutely no necessity for hauling grain all the way to Halifax by rail. I am utterly opposed to this vote.

Through the elevator yes;
I will give the figures.

Supply-Grain Elevators
